Hey Rishi — as we tighten up the Atlas ownership map, can you send me a quick read on:

- who owns each major area today;
- where urgent support issues should route, including the expected handoff;
- which support or code paths you still know best and should be pulled into for specific questions.

This is just to keep the map practical and make sure support has clear ownership—not to make you the default escalation path. A rough list is totally fine.

Retry and JWT expiry

1. Paginated query retry

If a paginated GraphQL request returns 401 because the JWT expired, refresh the JWT and replay the same page with the same opaque cursor. Do not advance the cursor or restart from the prior successful page.

```graphql
query {
  workspaceNodes(first: 50, after: "cursor_page_1") {
    nodes { id }
    pageInfo { endCursor }
  }
}
```

2. Mutation auth failure

If a mutation returns 401 before resolver execution, treat the request as having no side effect. After refreshing the JWT, the connector may replay the same mutation once; if refresh or replay fails, treat the request as failed.

```graphql
mutation {
  upsertConnectorCheckpoint(workspace: "wrk_test", seq: 42) {
    acknowledged
  }
}
```

3. Token-rotation headers

Unsupported token-rotation headers are outside the current GraphQL/JWT connector path; in-place token rotation is not a supported flow.

Finish the side-by-side first, then propose the copy change. Keep it copy-only and bounded to the source-owner path—no admin-model or permissions change.

Please compare the alternate against all four July clips and note:
- whether the visible source-owner cue is clear;
- whether the admin fallback is still accurate when the cue is absent;
- whether the wording gets users from workspace created to source connected/live sync without facilitator help.

If the evidence holds, bring the proposed wording and clip comparison back for review before changing it.

